The '''''geography of Sinchew''''' encompasses the physical features, terrain, and regional divisions of the country, which occupies approximately 2,020,000 km² (780,000 sq mi) of western [[Lurandia]]. Sinchew extends roughly 10.3 degrees of latitude from approximately 0.7°N to 11°S and 27 degrees of longitude, and is unusual among large countries in being almost entirely bounded by water: approximately 86.7 per cent of its national boundary meets the sea.

Three features dominate the country's physical geography. The first is a pronounced rise from west to east, in which coastal lowlands and gently sloping plains on the western margin give way to a broad central plateau carrying roughly a third of the national territory, and finally to an upland and highland belt stacked along the eastern edge. The second is that the northern portion of the country is the lowest, with mean elevations of only tens of metres, rising steadily southward. The third is the eastern highland belt, a single continuous range some 600 km long which forms the highest ground in the country and which is geologically distinct from the surrounding terrain.

== Location and boundaries ==

Sinchew occupies the western portion of the continent of [[Lurandia]], facing the open [[Shendan Ocean]] to its north and north-west. It is bounded by land on only part of its perimeter. Proceeding clockwise from the north, its neighbours are [[Balboa]] on a short north-eastern frontier, [[Amiera]] to the east, [[Etowah]] to the south and south-east, and [[Louyang]] to the south-west.

The country's land borders fall into two distinct classes. The southern border with Etowah and Louyang is a straight line running east–west at approximately 11°S for over 1,000 km, with no relationship to terrain. The eastern border with Amiera is likewise substantially straight over its northern portion. These are political boundaries of the kind associated with colonial partition, and they cut across the country's geological and physiographic structure — a fact of some importance for the interpretation of Sinchew's tectonics, since the geological units described below continue beyond the national frontier.

The western and northern coasts, by contrast, are entirely natural. Sinchew has no land boundary on its western or northern sides; the coastline there is the country's true edge.

The northernmost point of Sinchew lies on the northern coast near the [[Balboa]] frontier, while the southernmost and south-westernmost points lie on the straight-line southern border. The easternmost point lies on the coast facing Amiera.

== Topography ==

[[File:Sinchew topography labelled.png|thumb|300px|Broad topographic divisions of Sinchew]]
<!-- Image prompt: shaded-relief map of Sinchew with white label boxes marking the Western Lowlands, Northern Plain, Central Plateau, Eastern Uplands and Eastern Highlands -->

Sinchew's terrain is organised primarily along a west–east gradient, modified by a secondary north–south gradient that runs in the opposite sense across parts of the country.

Mean elevation across the national territory is 319 m, with a median of the same figure and a maximum of 2,697 m. The distribution is strongly bimodal: roughly a third of the country sits on a narrow band of elevation between 260 and 430 m, while a second large fraction occupies uplands between 560 and 800 m. Large areas of genuinely flat ground are characteristic of the interior, and relief is concentrated in relatively few places.

=== Western lowlands ===

The western margin of Sinchew, from the coast inland for roughly 200 to 400 km, consists of low ground generally below 260 m. This zone accounts for approximately 30 per cent of the national territory and includes the two lowest elevation classes. Mean elevation here is between 65 and 230 m depending on longitude, and the terrain is predominantly flat, with extensive areas of near-zero slope.

The western lowlands are widest in the north and narrow toward the south, where the coastline approaches the interior plateau more closely. Several of the country's larger water bodies lie within this zone.

=== Central plateau ===

Inland of the western lowlands, the terrain rises to a broad plateau occupying the band between roughly 260 and 430 m. This is the largest single physiographic division of the country, accounting for approximately 33 per cent of the national territory — a third of Sinchew lies within a single narrow elevation range.

The plateau is not a uniform surface. It forms a broad diagonal belt running from the north-west toward the south-east rather than following lines of latitude, and its western and eastern edges are stepped rather than gradual. Its surface is notably flat: over large areas the local relief is under a few tens of metres.

=== Eastern uplands ===

Beyond the plateau the terrain rises again through a belt of uplands between roughly 430 and 800 m, which accounts for a further 30 per cent of the country. This belt is widest in the centre of the country and narrows toward both the north and the south; it is essentially absent from the northern third of Sinchew, where the elevation classes above 430 m do not appear at all.

The uplands form the transition into the highlands proper, and their western edge constitutes one of the more sharply defined physiographic boundaries in the country.

=== Eastern highlands ===

The eastern highlands form the highest and most distinct terrain in Sinchew. They constitute a single continuous belt rather than a series of separate massifs, extending approximately 614 km from north to south and between 250 and 310 km from west to east, with a total area above 800 m of approximately 128,400 km². Mean elevation within the belt is approximately 1,450 m.

The crest of the highlands is not straight. It curves from a north-eastern position in the north of the country to a south-western one in the south, and the highest ground lies toward the southern half of the belt, reaching 2,697 m. Slopes within the highlands are the steepest in Sinchew, though still modest by international standards, with few areas exceeding 12 degrees.

Notably, the highlands terminate abruptly at the southern national border rather than declining gradually. This is an artefact of the boundary rather than a geological fact: the belt is truncated by the straight-line frontier with [[Etowah]] and [[Louyang]].

=== Northern plain ===

The northern third of Sinchew is anomalous within the country. Mean elevation falls to approximately 38 m between 1° and 2°S, and to 184 m between 2° and 3°S — the lowest ground in the country — and rises steadily southward thereafter to reach 548 m between 9° and 10°S. The elevation classes above 430 m are absent from this region entirely.

The northern plain therefore runs counter to the general western-lowland pattern. Where the rest of the country's low ground is found on its western margin, the lowest elevations in Sinchew are found in the north, irrespective of longitude.

== Hydrography ==

Sinchew possesses an extensive system of rivers and lakes. The national territory contains 191 major rivers with a combined mapped length of approximately 12,600 km, and 225 lakes of at least one km², with a combined surface area of approximately 39,300 km², or roughly 1.9 per cent of the national territory.

=== Rivers ===

The longest of the mapped rivers runs approximately 640 km, and the median length is approximately 47 km.

The mapped rivers represent only the principal courses. Extrapolation from them, using standard stream-ordering relationships, places the total number of discrete watercourses in the country at approximately 65,000, with a total channel length of roughly 3 million km. The great majority of these are short headwater streams that appear on no map.

Rivers fall into two classes according to the terrain they cross. The largest rise on the eastern uplands and highlands and descend westward, crossing every physiographic division in sequence on their way to the western or northern coast. The greatest total descent among the mapped courses is approximately 1,190 m, from a source above 1,350 m to a mouth near sea level.

A second class of river occupies the interior plateau and its margins, where gradients are extremely low. Several courses of several hundred kilometres descend only a few tens of metres, flowing across near-level ground.

=== Lakes ===

Sinchew's lakes are numerous but highly unequal in size. Of 225 lakes, only 47 exceed 100 km² and only seven exceed 500 km². The largest three account for a majority of total lake area.

The largest lake in Sinchew covers approximately 14,705 km², making it comparable in extent to a small sea. It lies in the northern interior in an area of low ground and is markedly elongated east–west, measuring roughly 477 km in that direction against only 144 km north–south — a ratio of more than three to one. It occupies the floor of a shallow trough, with higher ground both to its north and south.

The second largest lake, approximately 7,173 km² in area, lies far to the south in the interior plateau. Unlike the largest lake it is elongated north–south, measuring approximately 181 km against 109 km. The third largest, at approximately 1,970 km², shares this north–south orientation.

The distinctive orientation of the northern lake — east–west, in contrast to the north–south alignment of the others — reflects a structural control discussed below.

