The regions range in area from 358,539 km² to approximately 15,600 km². The largest single region, the Central Plateau under a tropical savanna climate, covers 358,539 km² and alone accounts for roughly 18 per cent of the national territory. Eight regions exceed 100,000 km²; the remaining twenty-two are smaller and more restricted in extent.

The regions fall into five broad groups. The coastal lowlands group occupies ground below 125 m along the western and northern margins, and is characterised by warm temperatures between 23 and 26 °C and high rainfall where it faces the open ocean. The lowlands group occupies the band between 125 and 260 m immediately inland, with similar temperatures but somewhat lower rainfall. The central plateau group, the largest by total area, occupies the 260 to 430 m band and includes the single largest region in the country; its members span the widest climatic range of any group, from tropical rainforest through savanna to humid subtropical. The uplands and upper uplands groups occupy the 430 to 800 m bands and are concentrated in the centre and south of the country, with markedly cooler temperatures around 19 to 21 °C. Finally, the eastern highlands group comprises a single region at the highest elevations in the country, with a median elevation of 1,519 m and a mean annual temperature of only 14.1 °C, the coolest and wettest terrain in Sinchew.

== Coastline ==

Sinchew's coastline is long and irregular, and accounts for approximately 86.7 per cent of the national boundary. Measured cell by cell it extends to roughly 20,000 km, though this figure reflects the highly crenulated character of the coast rather than a smooth linear distance.

Sinchew has frontage on two oceans, a distinction it shares with no other country in [[Lurandia]]. The greater part of its coast faces the [[Shendan Ocean]], the deep ocean that separates [[Lurandia]] from [[Fosia]]. The deepest point on Adonia, the [[Mariana Trench]], lies within Shendan waters. The Shendan coastline runs from the north-western corner of the country eastward along the northern margin, and is open water of considerable depth; the northern coast is consequently fully exposed to oceanic conditions.

The western and south-western coast faces the [[Gulf of Louyang]], an enclosed body of water lying between Sinchew and the landmass of [[Louyang]]. The gulf occupies the corner of the sea where Louyang's northern coast approaches Sinchew from the west and south-west, closing the water off from the open ocean at that end. Its coastal waters are shallower and less exposed than those of the Shendan, and the gulf provides Sinchew's principal sheltered coastline.

At the north-eastern corner of the country, a narrow north–south sliver of territory reaches far enough east to meet the [[Aethiopean Ocean]], giving Sinchew a short stretch of coast on a second ocean. The sliver is of geopolitical as well as geographic significance. It lies between [[Balboa]], which it abuts to the north-east, and [[Amiera]], which it abuts to the south-east. Because both territories meet the sliver rather than each other, Amiera and Balboa share no common boundary: Sinchew separates them along their entire mutual approach, which elsewhere narrows to approximately 20 km between their nearest points. Sinchew's Aethiopean coastline exists only because this territory was not absorbed by Amiera.

The eastern coast is short and largely truncate, following the straight-line political border with [[Amiera]]. Because that boundary is political rather than natural, this stretch of coast is defined by treaty rather than by any change in the character of the shore.

== Climate ==

{{main|Climate of Sinchew}}

Sinchew's climate is tropical and strongly maritime, dominated by the seasonal migration of the {{w|Intertropical Convergence Zone}} across the country and modified by the eastern highlands.

Annual precipitation ranges from approximately 795 mm in the western interior to 5,719 mm on the windward slopes of the eastern highlands, with a national median of 2,369 mm. Mean annual temperature ranges from 5.3 °C on the highest summits to 27.0 °C in the northern lowlands, with a median of 21.9 °C. No part of the country is arid.

The climate is organised along two gradients. Rainfall declines from north to south, producing a transition from {{w|tropical rainforest climate|tropical rainforest}} through {{w|tropical monsoon climate|monsoon}} to {{w|tropical savanna climate|savanna}} and finally {{w|humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical}} conditions in the far south. Superimposed on this is a strong orographic gradient: the eastern highlands intercept the prevailing easterly trade winds and receive the highest rainfall in the country, while the interior and western lowlands lie in their rain shadow.

The combination of the two gradients produces six {{w|Köppen climate classification|Köppen}} climate types, of which the tropical monsoon type is the most extensive at 34.8 per cent of the national territory. The {{w|subtropical highland climate}} of the eastern highlands is the only type in the country driven principally by elevation rather than latitude.

== Geology and tectonics ==

Sinchew lies on the [[Sinchew Subplate]], a small crustal block that is distinct from the main [[Lurandian Plate]] on which the greater part of the continent rests. The boundary between the two is marked by the [[Great Lurandian Mountain Range]], which runs roughly north–south through central Lurandia and whose western flank forms Sinchew's eastern highlands.

The interpretation of Sinchew's terrain rests on three observations.

First, the eastern highlands are a collisional suture. Their geometry — a single continuous belt 614 km long and up to 310 km wide — is characteristic of a continental collision zone rather than a volcanic arc or a rift shoulder, which would be narrower and, in the case of a rift, would occur in paired flanks. The belt is also intraplate: it lies far from any active plate margin, and no subduction is occurring nearby. It is therefore a relict orogenic belt, formed during an ancient collision and preserved because the crust beneath it remains thickened.

Second, the convergence is slow, and this explains the range's subdued character. The [[Lurandian Plate]] is the slowest-moving major plate on Adonia, advancing at approximately 21 mm per year. This is consistent with a range that has been decaying for hundreds of millions of years rather than actively rising: at 2,697 m, Sinchew's highlands are far lower than the alpine ranges produced by rapid convergence.

Third, the northern lake occupies a rift valley. The largest lake in the country is elongated strongly east–west and sits in a shallow trough with higher ground on both its northern and southern sides — a geometry consistent with a rift basin rather than a simple lowland. Its orientation across the general north–south trend of the country's structure indicates a separate, east–west extensional feature within the subplate: a rift arm that opened and then failed, leaving a trough that subsequently filled with water.

The [[Gulf of Louyang]] is connected to this history. It occupies a rift basin formed when the Sinchew Subplate separated from the landmass of Louyang to the west, and the east–west rift valley of northern Sinchew is interpreted as a failed arm of that same rift system — an extensional branch that opened eastward into the subplate but never reached full oceanic width. The subplate has therefore been compressed along its eastern margin, where the collision with the main Lurandian Plate raised the Great Lurandian Mountain Range, while extending along its western margin, where the gulf opened.

Because the country's southern and eastern borders are straight political lines rather than geological boundaries, both the Sinchew Subplate and the Great Lurandian Mountain Range continue beyond the national territory. The highlands in particular are truncated by the southern frontier, and the range is understood to extend further south and east into the interior of Lurandia than the portion within Sinchew.

